---
title: Tomato soup
description: Lesson 3.1. Sweating without browning, and a simmer that is not a boil.
servings: 4
time: 45 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, wet-heat]
lesson: "3.1"
technique: sweating and simmering
status: draft
---

> Technique: sweating, then simmering. Sweating softens vegetables in
> their own moisture with no browning. Simmering cooks in liquid that
> is below a full boil. Both are about keeping the temperature down.

Melt @butter{30%g} in a #large pot{} over medium-low heat. Add @onion{1}(finely chopped) and a pinch of salt and cook for ~{10%minutes}, stirring now and then, until the onion is soft and see-through but has not coloured. The pan should sound quiet.

> Why listen: while there is moisture in the pan the sound is soft and
> muffled. If it turns into a loud sizzle, the water has gone and the
> onion is about to brown: turn the heat down or add a spoonful of
> water.

> Science: while water is present, a pan cannot go above 100 °C, and
> the Maillard reaction, the browning of sugars with amino acids, only
> runs quickly at about 120 °C and above. Sweating keeps the onion wet
> on purpose, so it softens without browning. Look up:
> Maillard reaction, sweating (cooking).
-- source: Dan Souza and Alyssa Vaughn, "Want to Be a Better Cook? Listen to Your Food", America's Test Kitchen, 2023-03-10, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/7042-how-to-listen-to-food ("it won't go above 212 F"; browning "quickly at temperatures of 250 F or higher")

Add @garlic{2%cloves}(finely chopped) and stir for ~{1%minute}.

Add @tinned tomatoes{800%g} and @vegetable stock{500%ml}. Bring to a boil, then turn the heat down until only a few small bubbles break the surface. Simmer for ~{20%minutes}.

> Science: a boil and a simmer are different temperatures. At a boil
> the liquid is at 100 °C and large bubbles break the surface all the
> time. At a simmer it is at about 82 to 88 °C and small bubbles break
> the surface now and then. Less agitation means food is not knocked
> apart. Look up: simmering, boiling point.
-- source: "Cooking Class: Boil versus Simmer", Cook's Illustrated, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/cooksillustrated/how_tos/10660-cooking-class-boil-versus-simmer (boil 212 degrees; simmer 180 to 190 degrees)

Blend the soup with a #stick blender{} until smooth. Taste it, then add @salt{1/2%tsp} a pinch at a time, tasting between pinches, until the tomato tastes full. Stir in @cream{50%ml} if you like.

> Why season last: stock and tinned tomatoes vary a lot in salt, and
> simmering concentrates whatever is there. Salt the finished soup,
> not the pot you started with.
-- source: "7 Tips for Successful Soup Making", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-10-10, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/6226-7-tips-for-successful-soup-making

> Next time, without the recipe: sweat an onion until soft and quiet,
> add the main vegetable and a liquid, simmer with lazy bubbles, blend,
> and season at the end.
